okay this is let's begin with breaking  news from kyrgyzstan where security  services are saying they've foiled what  they're calling a coup attempt at least  15 people have been detained including  politicians and former officials will  al-jazeera zane bastravis in the capital  bishkek with more on what's happening  what more do we know about what's going  on  well rob what we're hearing is coming  from an official government statement  that was released  by the security service they say that in  tandem with the minister of internal  affairs they carried out a series of  raids in which they arrested 15 people  who were planning an armed coup in the  post-election period now this comes just  less than 48 hours before the country is  due to go to the polls  for parliamentary elections and the  reports suggest that what was going to  happen is that these were organizers  that were going to try to amass as many  as a thousand people in the days after  the election to contest elections not in  the halls of government but on the  street as is very common  in kyrgyzstan now what we were hearing  is that they were planning to have  clashes with police and security  services and try to use that as an  excuse to uh overthrow violently  president sader jabara's government  ironically president sadha japara of  just last year rose to power in similar  circumstances this country has seen  three violent revolutions in the last 15  years and upcoming parliamentary  elections are something that the  government is trying to use to show that  they are the adults in the room that  they are the government of stability and  this uh report of a foiled coup plot it  has to be said is happening at a very  convenient time just days before the  parliamentary vote  let's to talk about those upcoming  elections i mean where does the  opposition for example stand with regard  to to the elections  well  you know one thing that's happened  during the campaign season is that  president sader japarov uh he is  extremely popular but he's been  criticized for cracking down on those  that are against him as critics any sort  of political dissent as well as cracking  down on press freedom but the fact of  the matter is in the rural as well as  urban areas he is still the most popular  political leader currently in the  country  blinded by a childhood disease dastan  bekeshev struggled with depression at an  early age  the pursuit of knowledge he says saved  him and led him to a career as a lawyer  once a member of the party overthrown by  kyrgyzstan's president sadhar japarov  the acting mp is now running for  parliament as an independent  his campaign slogan i see the truth a  play on his disability suggests he has  not lost his sense of humor  and perhaps a jab at the current  leadership he may be blind but even he  is able to see the people's real  problems  social media is just like tv  you can be recognizable you can make  appeals from social media but you will  not lead your voters to the ballot boxes  that is why personal contact the moment  when you are chatting with your  potential voter it betters the chances  that this voter will come and vote for  you  in a system that has for years run on  political favoritism dastan walking the  beat makes him a rare breed  the election for a new parliament is  only a few days away but walking around  the capital bishkek you wouldn't be able  to tell this is the lowest level of  public activity this country has ever  seen before a vote now that may be down  to a nationwide case of what some  experts are calling political fatigue  this is after all the fourth round of  polls this country is conducting in a  year  political heat from opposing forces is  also noticeably milder dissent has been  muffled by allegations of corruption  opposition activists and leaders are out  of the country or in prison  and front runners that are likely to  fill parliamentary seats largely back  the president  japarov has not been as heavy-handed as  previous leaders but has cracked down on  political opponents intimidated critics  and passed laws to weaken press freedom  things he said he would not do  public promises that make him popular  his critics say are a cover for the same  corruption of past governments  many fear the elections will be followed  by hostility  i have no doubt that's going to happen  history's shown us that such dictators  as the current government wait for  elections only to strengthen their  vertical power as soon as they have  enough support as soon as they have the  parliament and the courts in their hands  they will intensify their fight against  us  voter turnout is going to be an  important indicator of the health of  democracy in kyrgyzstan  and after years of ballots cast out of  fear under pressure or for payment turn  out this time is expected to be  extremely low  zambasravi al-jazeera bishkek
 

